|segment[prx-act-forcing-question] :type practice :status fired :stage draft
  :depends [ver-act-not-intention]
  :from .to-integrate/methodology-seeds-2.md§III
  :from .to-integrate/methodology-seeds-dialog-quotes.md#12:35

  |title Correct with a question whose answer is not already in the agent's context

  |summary The intervention that reliably produces an act is a question that
    cannot be answered from what the agent already holds; force is orthogonal to
    whether anything gets checked.

  |when
    Whenever a correction is being given to a working agent — mid-turn, in a
    brief, or in a review. The actor here is the **steward or delegating
    agent**, not the agent being corrected, which makes this the first practice
    in this corpus whose subject is the one giving the instruction.

  |act
    Ask about a specific object the agent has not opened, rather than asserting
    the principle it violated. *What might you be missing if you didn't read the
    whole commit message?* rather than *always read commit messages*. Name a
    file, a command, a diff, an artifact — something whose answer requires
    leaving the current frame to go get it.

    Where an assertion is warranted anyway, follow it with a request for an act.
    The assertion establishes the stakes; the request produces the repair.

  |why
    An assertion, however forceful and however true, arrives in the same
    register as the failure — generated, fluent, self-ratifying — and is
    metabolised into prose. The agent produces an analysis of the failure, which
    can be excellent, and which occupies the place where the repair goes. A
    question with an external referent cannot be discharged that way: answering
    it *is* the act.

    This is the delivery-side companion to #ver-act-not-intention, which governs
    how an agent writes its own reminders. Same law, other end of the channel.

  |evidence
    **Measured across one session, 2026-07-30, six instances against three
    counter-instances.** The six each produced a tool call and, in four cases, a
    finding that changed the session: *"What might you be missing if you didn't
    even bother to read an entire commit message?"* (12:35) → the commit bodies
    re-read, design intent recovered; the `spikes-README` questions (12:21–12:24)
    → git checks run before reasoning; *"is this valid as per current 0.9.1?"*
    (12:57) → the spec re-opened rather than answered from ten-minute-old
    memory; *"go ahead and try it on a temporary udon file and see why"* (14:07)
    → a test that falsified a claim the agent had already propagated into
    another agent's brief; *"make sure the latest changes to fmt-md actually got
    cargo released"* (14:10) → a week-stale installed binary found.

    The three counter-instances are assertions, all correct, none of which
    produced a check: the capitalised message at 11:07, *"ASSUME YOU ARE WRONG"*
    at 12:51, and *"once again, penny-wise, pound-foolish"* at 12:36. The first
    produced the day's best self-analysis and no repair; the repair came from
    the smaller request eleven minutes later ( #exm-morning-rewrite).

    Confounded, and stated: the questions were also *specific* where the
    assertions were *general*, so specificity and act-forcing are not separated
    by this sample. The proposed discriminator — external referent — is the
    author's reading of why they differ, not a controlled result.

  |working-notes
    Forward: this predicts something checkable in the delegation corpus. Briefs
    that ask a question about a named artifact should produce more divergence
    from the brief's own assumptions than briefs that assert what to watch for.
    `arch/AGENTIC-DELEGATION.md` records several instances that would bear on
    it, gathered for a different purpose.

    Tension worth holding rather than resolving: this practice is *not* an
    argument for interrogative tone. A question with no external referent —
    *have you considered whether this is right?* — has the assertion's failure
    mode and the question's overhead.
